# Tokens the SAM address codecs actually look for.
#
# The base64 and base32 alphabets are recovered by the mutator on its own —
# they are byte-by-byte table lookups over a dense range, which is the one
# thing random mutation is good at. What it will not stumble on is *structure*:
# the certificate header 384 bytes into a destination, the suffixes SAM
# appends, and the padding that ends a base64 run.

# The certificate header: type byte, then a two-byte big-endian payload
# length. Type 0 (NULL) with a zero-length payload is what almost every
# destination on the network carries, and it is the boundary between the
# public destination and the private key blob behind it.
"\x00\x00\x00"
# Type 5 (KEY certificate), the other one in live use, with the payload
# lengths its variants declare.
"\x05\x00\x04"
"\x05\x00\x08"
# A payload length that runs past any plausible blob, which is the arithmetic
# the target is really about.
"\xff\xff"

# Suffixes SAM appends, and the ones we strip.
".b32.i2p"
".i2p"
"b32.i2p"

# Base64 padding, and the two characters that make I2P's alphabet its own.
"="
"=="
"-"
"~"

# A `SILENT=false` destination line is the blob followed by space-separated
# parameters; the cut happens at the first character outside the alphabet.
" FROM_PORT="
" TO_PORT="
"\x0a"
